# question: Which alarms provide information from a same sensing pathway?
#
# A "sensing pathway" is the actual TRACE an alarm's technical chain follows,
# from FunctionalUnit (the top-level sensing concept) down to wherever it
# terminates -- not a fixed set of tiers decided in advance. Two alarms
# share a pathway only if they terminate at the same depth with the same
# type at every tier reached: some traces stop at Sensor (a connectivity
# fault, e.g. "Monitor - SpO2 sensor uit" -- no Signal node exists at all),
# some stop at Signal (a quality fault, e.g. "PHILIPSMONITOR - Storing in
# SpO2" -- the sensor is enabled and producing a signal, just an unreliable
# one), and the rest resolve all the way to Metric. A fixed 3-tier signature
# (FunctionalUnit, Sensor, Metric) wrongly conflates the first two cases --
# both lack a Metric, but one lacks a Signal entirely and the other doesn't;
# these are different technical situations, not the same pathway. Grouping
# on the deepest reached tier, whichever it is, keeps them apart.
#
# Anchored on hasFunctionalUnit/hasSensor deliberately: alarms with no
# sensing component at all (a bare device- or component-level fault, e.g.
# "CARDIOHELP - Verbinding verbroken") have no sensing pathway to share and
# are correctly excluded here -- that is CQ5's question, not this one.
#
# Signal's own TYPE is not independently discriminating (every Sensor type
# in this corpus produces exactly one Signal type), but Signal's
# presence/absence is -- which is why it stays in the grouping key. Metric
# is the deepest tier that matters (SignalAnalysis type is coarser than
# Metric -- e.g. one ArterialPressureAnalysis step covers both metric:ABP
# and metric:ABP_Mean -- so grouping on it instead of Metric would
# re-introduce the exact conflation this query is designed to avoid).
#
# Known limitation, deferred: the three ECG-derived HeartRate archetypes
# (PHILIPSMONITOR - Extreme Tachy / HF hoog / Lage hartfreq.) carry the
# generic ontology classes mda:Sensor/mda:Signal instead of the specific
# sensor:ElectroCardioGraphy_sensor / signal:ECG_signal concepts used
# elsewhere for the same FunctionalUnit (e.g. "Niet te leren ECG") -- a gap
# in p50_annotated.csv's Sensor column for those three rows, not a query
# defect. It doesn't corrupt this result (the three still correctly share
# a trace with each other), only its readability.
#
# Asserted only -- no inference.ttl predicate is involved, so this does not
# require the OWL-RL closure.
#
# Note for reporting: matchCount can exceed the distinct-label count in the
# result (e.g. Volume_Minute: 4 alarms, 3 distinct labels) when the same
# label recurs as a separate mda:AlarmType at a different priority.

PREFIX mda: <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#>
PREFIX skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#>

SELECT ?fuClass ?sensorClass ?signalClass ?metricNotation
       (GROUP_CONCAT(DISTINCT ?label; separator=" | ") AS ?alarms)
       (COUNT(DISTINCT ?a) AS ?matchCount) WHERE {
  ?a a mda:AlarmType ; mda:hasLabel ?label ;
     mda:hasMessage/mda:triggeredBy/mda:hasFunctionalUnit ?fuNode .
  ?fuNode a ?fuClass ;
          mda:hasSensor ?sensorNode .
  ?sensorNode a ?sensorClass .
  OPTIONAL {
    ?sensorNode mda:sensorProducesSignal ?signalNode .
    ?signalNode a ?signalClass .
    OPTIONAL {
      ?signalNode (mda:analyzedBy/mda:producesSignal)*/mda:analyzedBy/mda:producesMetric ?metricNode .
      ?metricNode a ?metricConcept .
      ?metricConcept skos:notation ?metricNotation .
    }
  }
}
GROUP BY ?fuClass ?sensorClass ?signalClass ?metricNotation
HAVING (COUNT(DISTINCT ?a) > 1)
ORDER BY ?fuClass ?sensorClass ?signalClass
